Since I launched the site, I have had variations of the same question: Where can we read your fiction? \
I've spent the better part of my professional life happily positioning and promoting others. And yet, I can write all day, all night, but forget to write about my writing. Maybe I tell myself publishing isn't as urgent as writing and documenting.\

\
As you might notice from the excerpts below, my commitment in my early writing life, of which these pieces are reflective, was to write about what I observed in everyday life, without thinking about what the "right" thing to show was, or which theme would be most appealing.\

\
I'm specifically surfacing seven of these stories set in Lesotho, as my writing has since evolved to include other settings. I'm actively submitting these for publication, so I can't share the full stories here, but I thought it would be fun to share a taste of what I've written over the years. These remain dear to me, and I hope to point you to the full versions somewhere, in time. \
Alongside placing these stories, I'm also working toward publication of two novels and a nonfiction book about grief.\
\

---

**The River Knows** \
*Unpublished*\
\** \
Many longed to leave your small village, cowering like a squatter on the edge of a hill. Likhakeng looked misplaced, like the homesteads might at any minute tip over into debris of malikotoana pots, enamel crockery and sacks of khoahla maize. Young women desired to flee from marriages that could, as you walked home from fetching water or firewood, come tumbling on you like a Nigerian synagogue did one September afternoon. The young men left behind pitch-black rural nights, to endure long days baying for beauty they would never behold in the equally dark underbelly of the city of lights. Some young women dreamt of servitude in the city's suburbs. But none desired departure more than you. Away from the Seaka river, to no particular place. Just away.\

\
The day your aunt's red car snailed into the yard, you and your maroon suitcase stood on the red shiny stoep outside your parents' two-roomed huis. Rakhali had been one of those who left, not to be chained to a sewing machine in a Chinese factory in Maseru, no. She hadn't escaped from working in the family fields only to go and work in the boers' fields in the background of Cape Town. Rakhali left to be better.\

\
"How are you Tšeki?" She inched the small car so close you retreated a little into the grey brick house, convinced she wanted to drive right through the greying door.

---

**Not Compatible with Decency** \
*Unpublished* \
\
As the speakers ascend to their spot in the circus, Reitumetse thinks of a colleague who once told her that in Uganda, they flog corpses of people who die by suicide. Of course, Khahliso did not go that way. But that doesn't stop Reitumetse from fantasizing about flying off the mourning mattress on Friday when his body comes home and flogging him until he is deader than he is now. She could just see herself, flinging the blanket as far as someone her size could hurl a mink, dumping him out of that shiny 87,000 Maloti box she had picked from the funeral home catalog. She would beat the truth out of him. She would hurt him far more than that accident had.

---

**Predisposed** \
*Unpublished*\
\** \
’Mangoane almost dropped the enamel bowl of grave-diggers' meat nestled atop her head when she saw me. I called her ’Mangoane, mother’s sister, but the precise trajectory of our relation I could not readily articulate. She was most notorious for once biting off another relative's lower lip and refusing to give it back to be sewn on, so that years after their fight, the other woman descended to her grave missing her lower lip. Guarding me with her popped eyes as though I might mysteriously vanish, she theatrically lowered the bowl to the brown earth, her narrow lips stretched in a gap-toothed smile. \
\
She placed a stern kiss on my lips with her mouth only half closed. "We will talk later, child of my brother. See what you can do. There are pumpkins in the tent." \
\
Mangaone’s booming bottom up and downed as she made her way to the big *moluoane* tree under which the men gathered. \
\
I whipped out the newly purchased kitchen knife from my sling bag to plunge into the pumpkins on the metal table in the middle of the tent.

---

**The Plan** \
*Unpublished*\
\** \
Mzikababa's heart pounded heavily as the rain on the corrugated iron roof of the small room. On nights like this, Mr. Khomonngoe expected him to patrol his big yard ever more vigilantly. Understandably so, as criminals often took advantage of such weather. Yet, for the past few hours, his legs had failed to ferry him. The red wall clock's long black arm was lingering on one, the short on three. They seemed to have been stuck there forever. The night was dragging slowly by like the week before payday.\

\
Nthoamehla may have had a less than noble motive for making her midnight call, but she couldn't be faulted. "I didn't want to tell you this until I had seen with my own two eyes," she'd near whispered. "I first heard my boss saying a car had been dropping your wife off for the past few days. Tonight, when I woke up to get water from the bucket, I saw it for myself, a white car leaving her right at the door." Had she merely wanted to cause trouble, she never would have waited until she was sure before spilling all. Anyway, Nthoamehla was no *loskop*, unlike some of her predecessors.

---

**The Bang** \
*Previously published*\
\** \
She wore her yellow dress the day they came, a happy-eyed sunflower, carrot braids tied back with a brown headband. His uncle had called the night before to say they would come to the house to respond to the letter informing them of the "damage" to her by their son. Mosa just wanted the traditional formalities out of their way as she smoothed her thick eyebrows in front of her bedroom mirror. Their talks would give way for her to follow the natural path.\

\
The man whose child she was now carrying, unlike many, never expressed concern about her having had five-year-old Thuto before she met him. She and Moliko had been together for two years and with this "lucky accident," his family's response would be nothing but to propose a wedding between the two. She was ready, ready especially for the moment after their discussion when she would be called in to meet her future in-laws.

---

**Those Who Are Under Have Spoken** \
*Unpublished*\
\** \
"Speak to your wife Ntate," Rangoane Mohlolo egged, swinging the blanket to free his hands and gesture toward 'Matšotleho as though if he did not point at her, Moleko would not know which one of them was his wife. "Remember you called us here to support you, not to speak for you."\

\
"Yes, Rangoane," Moleko purred, like one who wanted to speak and to not speak at the same time.\

\
"Then speak, child of a man. Speak, son," Rangoane Monyane boomed, mouth freed from the blanket's cover and voice more voluminous than build, as always.\

\
"Those who are under have spoken, *mosali'aka.*"\

\
Moleko's intonation and how articulately he laid the Sesotho on his tongue shocked 'Matšotleho more than his mention of ancestors.\

\
She did not intend to sound sarcastic, but she did, "What have those who are under said?"

---

**My Family's Keeper** \
*Unpublished* \
\
When they tell you that as a married woman you must bow to your husband, they don't pick and choose to what and when you will bow. You bow. You don't even just bow to him. The day I got married, in the evening during litlhobohanyo, my own mother turned on me before the approving eyes of my husband's family. "If your new mother flies with a broom in the night, you join her. We don't want to see you running back home saying these people do this and that. Our home is no longer your home. You are now one of these people, and as they do, you will do."

Now, I feel like doing anything but bowing to this stranger as I stand to walk away, depleted.

---

*If you're an editor or publication and any of these speak to you, I'd love to hear from you.*
